```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListIndexOfX < 0);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount && New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
```--------------------The relevant csharp code in the form of Debug.Assert statements according to the comments is as follows:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);
```

This means if the index of x before contains more or equals elements during the operation, it will be a false condition which will fail the assertion. It might be that after calling the Contains() function on the array list, the x has been removed or the index of x changes in the array list.

Therefore, adding this Assert statement in the test method will ensure that the post condition of our test holds true. In the code above, `Assert( condition, string);` is used to check a condition in C#. If this condition is false, the program will raise an exception and print out the string.--------------------Here is the code for assert statements that start with "@@@":

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

// @@@ If the return value is false, index of x before is more than or equal to after
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false, "Index of x before is more than or equal to after");
```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the return value is true, Count before is less than or equal to after
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount <= New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If the return value is true, Last index of x is the same before and after or after is -1
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);

// @@@ If the return value is true, New_ret is the same before and after
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1);
```--------------------```cs
        { 
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 || Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
