```--------------------First, you need to install the NuGet package for Pex (Pexomatic Exploration) using .NET CLI:
```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.Pex.Framework
```
Then you can use Regex to extract all the comments starting with "@@@" and then use them as debug assert statements. Here is how you can do it in C#: 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string testCode = @"// @@@ If the return value is false, index of x before is more than or equal to after";
        string pattern = @"\/\/\s+@@@(.*?)";

        Regex rg = new Regex(pattern);
        Match match = rg.Match(testCode);

        if (match.Success)
        {
            string comment = match.Groups[1].Value;

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX, comment);
        }
    }
}
```
However, this example doesn't show how you can output the C# assertion code. As Pex operates at a method-level rather than a line-level, your CPE (Coverage, Performance, Ergonomics) testing will probably need to be at the method level or more closely controlled to fit into the test code. Pex handles that part at a code generation level which unfortunately isn't immediately available for the output.

As the comment "If the return value is false,..." cannot be converted to a valid assertion, the corresponding output can only be a string to make use of in your application. It doesn't provide any actual test. It would require to modify that string in the Assert function for Pex to actually work.--------------------```cs
// @@@ If the return value is false, the index of x before is more than or equal to the after.
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the return value is false, the last index of x before is more than or equal to the after
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the return value is true, the count before the operation is less than or equal to the after.
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount <= New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If the return value is false, the count before the operation must be less than the after.
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount < New_arrListCount);
```--------------------Here is the output assert statement corresponding to the comment that starts with "@@@":

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            Assert.IsTrue(!New_Ret || Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // This ensures that if the return value is false, the index of x before is more than or equal to after
        }
    }
}
```--------------------There are no assert statements mentioned in your code, the function will always return a boolean. So, the output after executing the function is a boolean type.--------------------Based on the comments in the code provided, it specifies that the output `Debug.Assert` statements should check that if the return value is `false`, then the index of `x` before the operation could not be less than the index after the operation. However, since there are many values `Old_arrListCount, Old_x, Old_arrListIndexOfX, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListCount, New_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX, and New_Ret` to check, it's not as simple as writing a simple `Debug.Assert(...)` statement.

However, since your requirement is to generate a `Debug.Assert` statement with a specific condition, without a solid understanding of what conditions to check, I'll focus on one of the conditions. You should adjust this as per the actual requirement.

Below is just an example of how you might get started extracting the debug.assert statements :

```csharp
// @@@ If the return value is false, index of x before is more than or equal to after
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= New_arrListCount || New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX || New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "Incorrect Return Value");
```

This is a basic assertion that checks if the count of the list is less than or equal to the newly checked count, or if the index of a given x has been increased by the operation, or if it has been decreased, the operation of Contains in ArrayList would be incorrect. You would need to adjust the conditions as per your requirements.

Remember in csharp "Assertions are methods which allow developers to insert extra checks into their code. By default, assertions are disabled in the release mode in C#, but you can enable them through your IDE settings." [Here is a simple guide](https://stackify.com/what-is-assertion-in-c/). These assertions are used to ensure the program is running correctly and have the correct output, they typically include a condition and a message.--------------------